#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# frobenius_automorphism

#% text
# `frobenius_automorphism(a)` gives `a^p` for an element `a` of a finite field of characteristic `p`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `frobenius_automorphism(a, k)` gives `a^(p^k)`; `k` is taken modulo the degree `n` of the field, so `k = n` gives `a` back.

#% item
# The Frobenius automorphism generates the Galois group of `F_(p^n)` over `F_p`; its fixed points are the prime field.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
F = finite_field(3, 3)

#% cell
a = F([1, 0, 2])

#% cell
frobenius_automorphism(a)
#% output
#= finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(1 + 2*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [0, 2, 2])

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Powers of the Frobenius:

#% cell
table(frobenius_automorphism(a, k), k, range(0, 3))
#% output
#= [finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(1 + 2*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [1, 0, 2]), finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(1 + 2*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [0, 2, 2]), finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(1 + 2*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [0, 1, 2]), finite_field_element(finite_field(3, &(1 + 2*&1 + &1^3), "Polynomial"), [1, 0, 2])]

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# It is multiplicative and fixes the prime field:

#% cell
b = F(11)

#% cell
[frobenius_automorphism(a * b) == frobenius_automorphism(a) * frobenius_automorphism(b), frobenius_automorphism(F(2)) == F(2)]
#% output
#= [true, true]

